WEBVTT

00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:03.060
A blueprint is created ahead of time,

00:00:03.300 --> 00:00:07.100
typically by a person. A coding assistant
can help write it.

00:00:07.380 --> 00:00:11.060
It defines which packages an agent’s
program can use,

00:00:11.180 --> 00:00:15.140
which actions are allowed, and which
trusted values your application

00:00:15.220 --> 00:00:19.380
must supply. Your application selects that
blueprint before

00:00:19.420 --> 00:00:20.740
the program runs.

00:00:22.000 --> 00:00:25.040
Start every blueprint with default deny,

00:00:25.160 --> 00:00:28.480
so anything not explicitly permitted is
denied.

00:00:28.800 --> 00:00:31.360
Listing a package lets the program import
it.

00:00:31.840 --> 00:00:35.960
Each protected capability still needs an
explicit allow rule.

00:00:36.240 --> 00:00:40.480
Put those rules under main, the section
that controls which operations

00:00:40.560 --> 00:00:42.560
the agent’s program can perform.

00:00:43.833 --> 00:00:48.153
A rule identifies an operation, the
conditions for allowing it,

00:00:48.233 --> 00:00:51.113
and what to do when they match. Here,

00:00:51.193 --> 00:00:55.513
a credit is allowed only for a premium
customer whose ID matches

00:00:55.553 --> 00:00:58.033
the customer ID bound to this session.

00:00:58.313 --> 00:01:02.153
The first matching rule decides. If no
rule matches,

00:01:02.193 --> 00:01:03.753
the request is denied.

00:01:05.033 --> 00:01:07.593
The program requests a customer ID.

00:01:08.153 --> 00:01:12.153
Your application supplies the authorized
customer ID from trusted

00:01:12.233 --> 00:01:15.033
information, such as the signed-in
account.

00:01:15.353 --> 00:01:17.993
Mark that value as required in the
blueprint.

00:01:18.473 --> 00:01:22.073
The generated program cannot change the
session’s binding.

00:01:23.367 --> 00:01:27.367
Bind Customer A’s ID, then request a
credit for that same

00:01:27.447 --> 00:01:30.087
premium customer: the rule matches.

00:01:30.327 --> 00:01:32.807
Request Customer B with that binding:

00:01:32.847 --> 00:01:36.647
the customer condition fails, so default
deny applies.

00:01:37.047 --> 00:01:39.767
The same blueprint decides both requests.

00:01:41.033 --> 00:01:43.913
A blueprint brings three choices together:

00:01:43.993 --> 00:01:47.113
available packages, allowed operations,

00:01:47.153 --> 00:01:50.873
and conditions such as which customer a
program may access.

00:01:51.353 --> 00:01:53.473
You define the rules ahead of time.

00:01:53.673 --> 00:01:56.313
Your application supplies trusted values.

00:01:56.633 --> 00:02:01.113
At each permission check, Submilli applies
those rules before the operation

00:02:01.193 --> 00:02:02.153
proceeds.
